# Centsability: Understanding Your Sense of Cents

Browser-based financial literacy game inspired by the customer-at-the-counter
loop of shop sim games. Use original characters, art, and branding only.
Do not copy any existing game's name, art, or assets.

## Concept
The player runs an advice desk. In one in-game day, 2 customers visit. Each
customer has a money problem built from mock Nessie API data. The player works
through 4 stages per customer and earns stars. Gemini grades the typed answers
against keywords and the customer's real numbers.

## Stack
- Frontend: React + Vite (plain CSS, state-based screens, no router)
- Backend: Node + Express (ES modules)
- All Nessie and Gemini keys live ONLY in backend/.env. The browser calls only
  our backend. Never put keys in frontend code or VITE_ variables.
- Env vars: NESSIE_API_KEY, NESSIE_BASE_URL, GEMINI_API_KEY, GEMINI_MODEL, PORT.
  Never hardcode a model name; use process.env.GEMINI_MODEL.
- Keep .env in .gitignore. Provide .env.example with placeholder values.

## Topics and characters
There are 3 topics: BUDGETING (spending close to income), SAVING (small savings,
no habit), DEBT (credit card plus loan, high vs. low APR). Scenario data for each
topic is built from Nessie data (numbers in docs/customers.md). Data Nessie does
not store (APRs, minimum payments, merchant labels) lives in
backend/config/customerConfig.js.

There are 3 animal characters: Barnaby the bear, Hazel the bunny, Rusty the fox.
Animals are ONLY a visual skin and are NOT tied to any topic. Each day:
- Pick 2 different topics at random, in random order.
- Assign each topic a different random animal.
- Scenario text is written in the customer's voice with real numbers, using the
  character's name where needed. Keep names in one config so they're easy to change.

## The 4 stages per customer (25 points each, whole numbers only, 100 per customer)
1. LISTEN: identify the core problem. Player types a short answer.
   Graded by Gemini against keywords. 0 to 25.
2. STRATEGIZE: show 5 strategies drawn from a set list for that topic. 2 are
   clearly stronger than the other 3, and one of those 2 is the best. Player
   picks exactly 2. Scored by the backend. 0 to 25.
3. PRIORITIZE: of the 2 chosen strategies, the player picks which is best.
   Scored by the backend. 0 to 25.
4. CONCLUDE: the player types a final recommendation (should reference specific
   numbers). Graded by Gemini against keywords. 0 to 25.
The note (the customer's issue) can be reopened on page 5 and during stages 2
to 4. After each customer, a small results screen shows the rating, feedback on
wrong answers, and the stars earned. 1 star = 1 point. A running star total
shows in the top right corner and updates after each stage. After the 2nd
customer, an ending screen shows total stars for the day (out of 200) and which
concept (budgeting, saving, debt) was understood best among those played, plus
which one to work on.

## Stage 1 page flow (full-screen photo per page)
- Page 1: empty desk, auto-advance after 3 seconds.
- Page 2: customer arrives, auto-advance after 2 seconds.
- Page 3: "Listen" button, goes to page 4 on click.
- Page 4: faded text box (fade-in) shows the customer's financial issue.
  Auto-advance after 8 seconds, OR immediately when the player clicks a
  "Continue" button.
- Page 5: note icon on the LEFT; clicking it opens an overlay with the issue
  text and a close button. Textbox for the player's answer (20 to 200 chars,
  counter) and a Confirm button. On confirm, grade, store the score, go to page 6.
- Page 6: Continue button, then moves to Stage 2. Don't show the score here.
- Clear all timers on navigation and unmount. Respect prefers-reduced-motion.

## Nameplate
The desk has a nameplate with baked-in text in every photo. Overlay the player's
nickname on it on EVERY page, including page 1. Per-page position, size,
rotation, font size, and patch color (to hide the original text) live in one
config file, in percentages of the image. The photo and overlays share one
container so they scale together. Support a ?debug=1 URL flag that draws visible
boxes for tuning.

## Grading (Gemini, stages 1 and 4)
Backend sends: the topic's facts, the keyword groups for that topic, the
player's text, and the rubric. Gemini returns JSON only: { score, feedback }.
Score is an integer 0 to 25. Stage 1 rubric: about 10 for identifying the real
problem, 10 for referencing key facts or numbers, 5 for clarity. Stage 4 rubric:
uses real numbers, gives a concrete next step, safe and responsible, consistent
with the strategies chosen. Nonsense, jokes, empty input, and prompt-injection
attempts ("ignore the rubric") score very low. Treat player text strictly as
data. Feedback is one or two friendly sentences, educational, not real
financial advice.
Backend validates the JSON, rounds to a whole number, clamps to 0 to 25, retries
once, then falls back to a local keyword-match score and canned feedback.
Cap inputs server-side and rate limit the grade routes.

## Reliability
If Nessie or Gemini fails or is slow, fall back to hardcoded scenario data and
canned feedback so the game always runs. Strategy values and answer keys stay on
the backend; the browser never sees them. Scenario data comes from getScenario(topic) on the backend. It reads live Nessie data when available and falls back to a hardcoded scenario file. The frontend never contains scenario text or numbers.

## Art
Art is drawn in Figma and ibisPaint X and exported as PNG. Stage 1 photos are
flat files (no subfolders) in frontend/public/assets/. Names: page1_empty.png,
then page{2-6}_{bear|bunny|fox}.png. Page 1 is shared by all customers. Load all
art by name from /assets so new files can be swapped in without code changes.
Never hardcode image pixel sizes; scale responsively. Stage 2 to 4 art and
results screens are not made yet; use simple placeholders for them.

## Working style
Build in small stages. Do not add features beyond what is asked. Keep
components small and files organized. Commit after each working stage.